# Validation Instructions — RBAC for `secrets-reader`

This change adds a `ServiceAccount`, `Role`, and `RoleBinding`
(`security/rbac.yml`) that grant permission to **list** `Secret` objects in
the `todoapp` namespace, and wires the `todoapp` `Deployment`
(`.infrastructure/app/deployment.yml`) to run as that `ServiceAccount` via
`spec.template.spec.serviceAccountName: secrets-reader`.

## 0. Create the cluster

A `kind` cluster config is provided at `cluster.yml` (1 control-plane + 4
worker nodes, with port mappings for the app's NodePort service).

```bash
kind create cluster --config cluster.yml
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes
```

> If you're using Docker Desktop's built-in Kubernetes instead of `kind`,
> just make sure Kubernetes is enabled (Docker Desktop → Settings →
> Kubernetes → Enable Kubernetes) and skip this step.

## 1. Apply the manifests

Apply MySQL first (the app depends on it), then the app manifests, then
RBAC. Order matters because `todoapp` and `mysql` are in separate
namespaces created by their own manifests.

```bash
# MySQL (its own namespace, StatefulSet, Secret, ConfigMap, Service)
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/mysql/

# App namespace + storage + config + secret first
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/ns.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/pv.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/pvc.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/secret.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/configMap.yml

# RBAC (ServiceAccount, Role, RoleBinding) — must exist before the Deployment
kubectl apply -f security/rbac.yml

# App workload + networking
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/deployment.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/clusterIp.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/nodeport.yml
kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/app/hpa.yml
```

> If `kubectl apply -f .infrastructure/mysql/` or `.infrastructure/app/`
> (applied as a whole folder) reports `namespaces "mysql"` or
> `"todoapp" not found`, it means files were applied out of order in one
> pass. Simply re-run `kubectl apply -f <folder>/` a second time — it's
> idempotent and will pick up whatever failed on the first pass once the
> namespace exists.

## 2. Confirm everything is running

```bash
kubectl get pods -n mysql
kubectl get pods -n todoapp
```

Both `mysql-0`/`mysql-1` and the `todoapp-*` pods should reach
`1/1 Running`. A restart or two while MySQL finishes starting up is normal;
watch with `kubectl get pods -n todoapp -w` if they're still cycling.

## 3. Confirm the RBAC objects exist

```bash
kubectl get serviceaccount secrets-reader -n todoapp
kubectl get role secrets-reader-role -n todoapp
kubectl get rolebinding secrets-reader-binding -n todoapp
```

Inspect the Role to confirm it only allows `list` on `secrets`:

```bash
kubectl get role secrets-reader-role -n todoapp -o yaml
```

Expected `rules`:

```yaml
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["secrets"]
    verbs: ["list"]
```

## 4. Confirm the Deployment uses the ServiceAccount

```bash
kubectl get deployment todoapp -n todoapp \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.serviceAccountName}'
```

Expected output: `secrets-reader`

## 5. Verify permissions with `kubectl auth can-i`

```bash
kubectl auth can-i list secrets \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:todoapp:secrets-reader \
  -n todoapp
# expected: yes
```

Negative checks (the ServiceAccount should NOT be able to do more than
this):

```bash
kubectl auth can-i get secrets \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:todoapp:secrets-reader \
  -n todoapp
# expected: no

kubectl auth can-i list secrets \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:todoapp:secrets-reader \
  -n default
# expected: no
```

## 6. Exec into the pod and curl the Kubernetes API to list secrets

Get the running pod name:

```bash
POD=$(kubectl get pod -n todoapp -l app=todoapp -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo $POD
```

Open an interactive shell in the pod:

```bash
kubectl exec -n todoapp -it "$POD" -- sh
```

Inside the pod's shell, run:

```sh
TOKEN=$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)
curl -s --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/todoapp/secrets
```

Expected result: a JSON response with `"kind": "SecretList"` whose `items`
array contains the `app-secret` object. This confirms the request succeeded
(not a `403 Forbidden`) using only the permissions granted by
`security/rbac.yml`.

Exit the pod shell when done:

```sh
exit
```

> **Windows/PowerShell note:** passing a multi-line or `$`-containing
> command straight into `kubectl exec ... -- sh -c '...'` from PowerShell
> is unreliable because PowerShell's quoting/escaping rules differ from
> POSIX shells. The interactive-shell approach above (`kubectl exec -it
> $POD -- sh`, then typing the two commands inside the pod) avoids that
> problem entirely and is the recommended way to reproduce this on Windows.

**This is the output to screenshot** — the terminal showing both commands
and the resulting `SecretList` JSON — and attach to the pull request as
evidence that the `secrets-reader` ServiceAccount can list secrets from
within the `todoapp` Deployment's own pod.

## 7. Clean-up (optional)

```bash
kubectl delete -f .infrastructure/app/
kubectl delete -f security/rbac.yml
kubectl delete -f .infrastructure/mysql/
kind delete cluster --name kind
```